Today's close comes down to two key takeaways:
(1) S&P/DOW both at all-time highs, yet only NASDAQ turned negative — the
    Broadcom-specific issue failed to dampen the broader market's advance.
    The fact that NVDA closed +1.82% on the same day is the strongest
    fact-check that AI infrastructure demand is intact. Generalizing Broadcom
    weakness to a chip cycle peak is poorly supported.
(2) GOOGL +3.68% lifted cost-return to +21.76%, recovering the 20d -6.5%
    weakness with a single catalyst (Palantir partnership + $80B raise) —
    suggesting big tech consensus flow remains resilient. However, 3 holdings
    simultaneously at +20%+ implies accumulating profit-taking pressure, so
    rather than adding, it is recommended to pre-define staged profit-taking
    triggers (e.g., VIX breaks 20, 10Y retakes 4.5%).

Per ADVISOR.md rule (no new buys if cash < 40%), overseas account cash of
32.1% is below the threshold, so new buys are on hold. Maintaining current
positions is the objective choice.
